#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222352} :activity/id #uuid "fbdb5c2f-7049-5754-be9f-35cca4bf3884" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049050 :topic/id #uuid "d5d3e7a8-b91e-501f-841f-df61b10e029f" :topic/math-academy-id 6372} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222478 :tutorial/content "Suppose a quantity increases over time at a rate proportional to its current value, such as the balance of a bank account growing with continuous interest. The Laplace transform can be used to analyze how such exponential growth behaves, especially when the function is **integrated over time** to represent total accumulation.\n\nTo illustrate this, we’ll find the Laplace transform of a function defined as the integral of an exponential function. This shows how *integration in the time domain* corresponds to *division by $s$* in the $s$-domain.\n\nSuppose we are given that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s>a.\n$$\n\nLet's find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\int_{0}^{t} e^{\\tau/2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau,\n\\qquad s > \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe apply the **Integration in Time Property**: if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\!\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=e^{t/2}$. Thus, for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{t/2}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-\\frac{1}{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have for $s>\\dfrac{1}{2},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\,\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ/2}\\,dτ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s\\,(s-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s(2s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ee57f084-2127-562e-bf7b-91641db97ba1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14359 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e6f15f22-f7c7-5bf4-a482-3a5056a94c4b" :step/math-academy-id 599887 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222353}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222525 :tutorial/content "Assume that\n\n- $f(t)$ is *piecewise continuous* on $[0,\\infty)$, and\n\n- $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a$.\n\nThen the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for all $s>a$, and we have the **Integration Property**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\!\\left\\{\\int_0^t f(\\tau)\\,\\textrm{d}\\tau\\right\\}\n= \\frac{F(s)}{s},\n\\qquad s>a.\n$$\n\nThis result shows that *integrating a function with respect to time* corresponds to *dividing its Laplace transform by $s$*.  \n\nThis result allows us to handle accumulated quantities without re-integrating from scratch.\n\nNext, we will apply this property to a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "05dac555-11de-5a37-a12e-e9c6ad069388" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14729 :tutorial/title "The General Result"} :step/id #uuid "56307df4-da62-5ba2-8afa-b2b66440392e" :step/math-academy-id 599888 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222354}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221157 :question/id #uuid "11b5421d-e98b-5e37-9bdd-bec13f010118" :question/math-academy-id "e-21503"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050200}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442020}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"} :step/id #uuid "a43eec6c-67d4-51d6-8cd3-dbcb10285abe" :step/math-academy-id